WEBVTT

00:00:03.110 --> 00:00:08.060
The sixth conversation is about change: how do we learn and reform?

00:00:08.110 --> 00:00:10.020
Teams are never finished.

00:00:10.070 --> 00:00:13.220
People join and leave. Priorities change.

00:00:13.270 --> 00:00:15.640
The organisation reorganises.

00:00:15.690 --> 00:00:19.040
What worked six months ago may be in the way today.

00:00:19.090 --> 00:00:25.460
A team that never stops to look at itself slowly drifts, and nobody notices until it hurts.

00:00:25.510 --> 00:00:36.060
Signs your team needs this conversation: something has changed, like a new team member, a new leader or a new strategy, and you haven’t talked about what it means for the team.

00:00:36.110 --> 00:00:39.800
Agreements from earlier sessions are quietly being ignored.

00:00:39.850 --> 00:00:43.080
The team feels tired, or stuck in habits.

00:00:43.130 --> 00:00:47.620
Or it’s simply been more than six months since you last looked at your canvas.

00:00:47.670 --> 00:00:50.880
The output on the canvas is three short lists.

00:00:50.930 --> 00:00:53.460
Keep: what should we protect?

00:00:53.510 --> 00:00:56.440
Change: what is no longer helping us?

00:00:56.490 --> 00:01:00.140
Try: what small experiment will we run next?

00:01:00.190 --> 00:01:04.520
And one more thing: what should make us sit down with the canvas again?

00:01:04.570 --> 00:01:08.000
This conversation can stand on its own, like a retrospective.

00:01:08.050 --> 00:01:12.080
But it’s most powerful when you use it to look back at the other five.

00:01:12.130 --> 00:01:15.620
Walk past the canvas: is our purpose still true?

00:01:15.670 --> 00:01:17.680
Do our decision rules still work?

00:01:17.730 --> 00:01:19.760
Is our contract still alive?

00:01:19.810 --> 00:01:23.799
In that way, the sixth conversation is what makes the other five last.

